// Class 11 Political Science practice bank, part 2: Federalism to Freedom (Indian Constitution at Work chapters 7-10, Political Theory chapters 1-2).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "polsci", {
  federalism: {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Defence, foreign affairs, banking and currency all appear in the:", options: ["State List", "Concurrent List", "Union List", "Residuary List"], answer: 2, explain: "These are subjects of national importance placed in the Union List of the Seventh Schedule, so only Parliament makes laws on them.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these subjects is on the State List?", options: ["Marriage", "Trade unions", "Railways", "Public health"], answer: 3, explain: "Police, agriculture, public health and local government are State List subjects; railways are on the Union List and marriage and trade unions on the Concurrent List.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Both Parliament and state legislatures can make laws on:", options: ["foreign affairs", "education", "defence", "currency"], answer: 1, explain: "Education is on the Concurrent List (moved there from the State List by the 42nd Amendment); the other three are Union List subjects.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The body set up under Article 280 to recommend how taxes are shared between the Union and the states is the:", options: ["Inter-State Council", "Sarkaria Commission", "Election Commission", "Finance Commission"], answer: 3, explain: "The Finance Commission under Article 280 recommends the sharing of tax revenue between the Union and the states.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Cauvery water dispute is between:", options: ["Karnataka and Tamil Nadu", "Maharashtra and Karnataka", "Assam and Nagaland", "Gujarat and Rajasthan"], answer: 0, explain: "Cauvery is the standard example of a river-water dispute between Karnataka and Tamil Nadu; the Belgaum issue is a border dispute between Maharashtra and Karnataka.", level: 1 },

      { q: "The States Reorganisation Act, 1956 is associated with:", options: ["transfer of education to the Concurrent List", "the introduction of President's Rule", "the reorganisation of states largely on linguistic lines", "the creation of the Inter-State Council"], answer: 2, explain: "Linguistic states were created under the States Reorganisation Act, 1956.", level: 2 },
      { q: "When Parliament proposes under Article 3 to change the name of a state, the views of that state are:", options: ["binding on Parliament", "sought but not binding", "required through a two-thirds vote of the state assembly", "not to be heard at all"], answer: 1, explain: "The state's views are sought, but Parliament is not bound by them. This is one reason the Centre is called strong.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): States in India have no right to secede from the Union.\nReason (R): The Indian federation was not formed by an agreement among the states.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Article 1 calls India a 'Union of States' because the federation did not come about through an agreement among states, so there is no right to secede.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: Two neighbouring states quarrel over the sharing of water of a river that flows through both, and each wants the Centre to take its side. Which Article 263 body is meant as a forum for Centre–state cooperation on such issues?", options: ["Finance Commission", "Governor's office", "Inter-State Council", "Election Commission"], answer: 2, explain: "The Inter-State Council (Article 263), along with zonal councils, provides a forum for discussion between the Centre and the states.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A state legislature passes a bill, but the Governor does not give assent and sends it to the President for consideration. This power illustrates:", options: ["the residuary powers of the states", "judicial review of state laws", "the right of states to secede", "the hold of the Union over states, since Governors can reserve state bills for the President"], answer: 3, explain: "Governors are appointed by the President and can reserve state bills for the President, one of the features that make the Centre strong.", level: 2 },

      { q: "A state government is dismissed under Article 356 on the ground that it has lost its majority, but the Chief Minister claims that the majority exists. According to the S.R. Bommai ruling (1994), the correct position is that:", options: ["the Governor's report is final and cannot be examined", "the majority should be tested on the floor of the House, and the President's Rule can be judicially reviewed", "the Supreme Court appoints the new Chief Minister", "the President alone decides, with no scrutiny"], answer: 1, explain: "The Supreme Court held that President's Rule is subject to judicial review and that a government's majority is to be tested on the floor of the House.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Residuary powers in India belong to the Union.\nReason (R): The Constitution places all subjects not mentioned in any list in the State List.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true (Article 248), but R is false: subjects not in any list go to the Union, not to the states.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which statement best explains why Article 371 and Articles 371A–371J are cited as examples of asymmetric federalism?", options: ["All states receive identical powers under them", "They allow certain states to opt out of the Union List", "They transfer residuary powers to some states", "They give some states special provisions because of their history, culture or geography"], answer: 3, explain: "Asymmetric federalism means that some units get special provisions, as under Article 371 and 371A–371J (e.g. for Nagaland, Mizoram and Sikkim).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Parliament and a state legislature pass conflicting laws on marriage. Separately, a new subject that no list mentions needs to be legislated upon. Which is correct?", options: ["Union law prevails on marriage; the Union has power over the new subject", "State law prevails on marriage; the states can legislate on the new subject", "State law prevails on marriage; the Union legislates on the new subject", "Union law prevails on marriage; the states legislate on the new subject"], answer: 0, explain: "Marriage is on the Concurrent List where Union law prevails in a conflict, and residuary subjects belong to the Union under Article 248.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What are residuary powers? In whom are they vested in India?", a: "Residuary powers are powers over subjects that are not mentioned in any of the three lists of the Seventh Schedule. In India they are vested in the Union (Parliament) under Article 248.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Which Article describes India as a 'Union of States'?", a: "Article 1 says 'India, that is Bharat, shall be a Union of States'; the word 'federation' is not used.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name any two subjects each from the Union List and the State List.", a: "Union List: defence, foreign affairs (also banking, currency, railways).\nState List: police, agriculture (also public health, local government).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State any two reasons why the makers of the Constitution wanted a strong Centre.", a: "1. The experience of Partition and the need to protect national unity.\n2. The need to integrate the princely states, and to carry out planned socio-economic development under a strong central government.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain three sources of conflict in Centre–state relations in India.", a: "1. Demands for autonomy: states have sought more financial and administrative powers, as raised by parties such as the DMK and the Akali Dal.\n2. Role of Governors and the use of Article 356 to dismiss state governments, sometimes for political reasons.\n3. Inter-state disputes over borders (e.g. Belgaum between Maharashtra and Karnataka) and river waters (e.g. Cauvery between Karnataka and Tamil Nadu).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What did the Sarkaria Commission recommend, and what did the Supreme Court decide in the S.R. Bommai case?", a: "1. The Sarkaria Commission (1983) examined Centre–state relations; it recommended impartial Governors and sparing use of Article 356.\n2. In the S.R. Bommai case (1994) the Supreme Court held that President's Rule under Article 356 is subject to judicial review.\n3. It also held that the majority of a state government must be tested on the floor of the House, not decided by the Governor's opinion.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What is asymmetric federalism? Give an example and a reason for it.", a: "1. Asymmetric federalism is a federal arrangement in which some units get special provisions that others do not.\n2. Example: Articles 371 and 371A–371J give special provisions to states such as Nagaland, Mizoram and Sikkim, e.g. protecting tribal customs and land in the north-east.\n3. Reason: the states differ in history, culture and geography, so one uniform arrangement would not suit all of them.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Examine the main tensions in Centre–state relations in India, with examples.", a: "1. Federal design: the Constitution created a strong Centre, so states depend on the Union for grants and loans and fear loss of autonomy.\n2. Autonomy demands: states and regional parties such as the DMK and the Akali Dal have asked for more financial and administrative powers.\n3. Governors and Article 356: Governors are seen as agents of the Centre, and President's Rule has been used to dismiss state governments. The Sarkaria Commission asked for impartial Governors, and the Bommai case (1994) made President's Rule open to judicial review and required floor tests.\n4. New states: demands for states led to the States Reorganisation Act (1956) and later to Chhattisgarh, Uttarakhand and Jharkhand (2000) and Telangana (2014).\n5. Inter-state disputes over borders (Belgaum) and river waters (Cauvery) need to be resolved through forums such as the Inter-State Council (Article 263) and zonal councils.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "'Federalism alone does not guarantee national unity.' Discuss with examples.", a: "1. Federalism divides power between a central and regional governments, with a written constitution and an independent judiciary to settle disputes.\n2. The USSR had a federal structure but broke up in 1991, so federalism alone did not hold it together.\n3. Canada has managed tensions such as those over Quebec, showing that federal arrangements can accommodate regional demands.\n4. In India, federalism recognises regional diversity while keeping the country united: states cannot secede, the Union has strong powers and special provisions such as Article 371 respond to regional needs.\n5. Cooperation bodies such as the Finance Commission, Inter-State Council and zonal councils also help to keep Centre and states together. Unity depends on how fairly power and resources are shared, not on the federal label.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: After an inconclusive election in a state, the Governor invited the largest party to form the government. Soon after, the Union government dismissed the state government under Article 356, saying that its majority had been lost. The state government challenged the decision in court, arguing that its majority should be tested in the Assembly.\n(i) Which Article deals with President's Rule?\n(ii) What did the Supreme Court say in the S.R. Bommai case about such a situation?\n(iii) Why is the role of the Governor often criticised?", a: "(i) Article 356.\n(ii) In S.R. Bommai (1994) the Court held that President's Rule is subject to judicial review and that a government's majority must be tested on the floor of the House.\n(iii) Governors are appointed by the President, effectively by the Union government, so they are seen as agents of the Centre; they have been accused of bias in inviting parties to form governments and in reserving state bills, and their reports can lead to President's Rule.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "local-governments": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The 73rd Amendment added which Part to the Constitution?", options: ["Part IXA", "Part III", "Part IX", "Part IV"], answer: 2, explain: "Part IX deals with panchayats (73rd Amendment); Part IXA, added by the 74th Amendment, deals with municipalities.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Twelfth Schedule, added by the 74th Amendment, lists how many subjects for municipalities?", options: ["29", "18", "21", "20"], answer: 1, explain: "The Twelfth Schedule has 18 subjects for municipalities; the Eleventh Schedule has 29 for panchayats.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Panchayati Raj system was first launched on 2 October 1959 at:", options: ["Wardha, Maharashtra", "Sabarmati, Gujarat", "Anand, Gujarat", "Nagaur, Rajasthan"], answer: 3, explain: "It was launched at Nagaur in Rajasthan, following the Balwant Rai Mehta Committee's recommendation of 1957.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Gram Sabha consists of:", options: ["all adult voters of the village panchayat area", "only the sarpanch and elected ward members", "only the members of the Panchayat Samiti", "only landowning families of the village"], answer: 0, explain: "The Gram Sabha is made up of all adult voters registered in the area of a village panchayat.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Under the 73rd and 74th Amendments, elections to local bodies must be held every:", options: ["3 years", "4 years", "5 years", "6 years"], answer: 2, explain: "Local bodies have a five-year term, and elections are conducted by the State Election Commission.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A state has a population of 15 lakh. Under the 73rd Amendment, this state:", options: ["must have all three tiers of Panchayati Raj", "may choose not to have the intermediate tier", "cannot have panchayats", "must have only the district tier"], answer: 1, explain: "States with a population below 20 lakh may skip the middle (block) tier.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A fast-growing area changing from rural to urban in character is governed by a:", options: ["Municipal Corporation", "Gram Panchayat", "Zila Parishad", "Nagar Panchayat"], answer: 3, explain: "A Nagar Panchayat governs an area in transition from rural to urban; a Municipal Council serves a smaller urban area and a Municipal Corporation a larger one.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The Gram Sabha is a mandatory body under the 73rd Amendment.\nReason (R): The Gram Sabha consists only of the elected sarpanch and ward members.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true, but R is false: the Gram Sabha includes all adult voters of the village panchayat area, not just elected representatives.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A gram panchayat has 30 seats to be filled by direct election. What is the minimum number of seats that must be reserved for women under the Constitution?", options: ["6", "15", "12", "10"], answer: 3, explain: "At least one-third of the seats are reserved for women: 30 ÷ 3 = 10. Some states reserve one-half.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A state government dissolves a district panchayat 18 months before its five-year term would end. According to the 73rd Amendment, fresh elections must be held:", options: ["within 6 months", "within 1 year", "within 3 months", "only at the end of the original five-year term"], answer: 0, explain: "If a body is dissolved early, elections must be held within six months.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Which sequence correctly orders these events in the growth of local government in India?", options: ["Balwant Rai Mehta Committee; Community Development Programme; L.M. Singhvi Committee; Nagaur launch; 73rd Amendment", "L.M. Singhvi Committee; Balwant Rai Mehta Committee; Community Development Programme; Nagaur launch; 73rd Amendment", "Community Development Programme (1952); Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957); Nagaur launch (1959); L.M. Singhvi Committee (1986); 73rd Amendment (1992)", "Community Development Programme; Nagaur launch; Balwant Rai Mehta Committee; 73rd Amendment; L.M. Singhvi Committee"], answer: 2, explain: "The Community Development Programme (1952) came first, then the Mehta Committee (1957), the Nagaur launch (1959), the Singhvi Committee (1986) and the 73rd Amendment (1992).", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Despite their constitutional status, local bodies in many states remain weak.\nReason (R): Many states have not transferred the listed subjects, funds and staff to local bodies.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Without real transfer of subjects, funds and staff, local bodies mostly implement schemes designed by higher governments.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which pairing is correct?", options: ["74th Amendment – Part IX – Twelfth Schedule", "73rd Amendment – Part IXA – Eleventh Schedule", "74th Amendment – Part IXA – Eleventh Schedule", "73rd Amendment – Part IX – Eleventh Schedule"], answer: 3, explain: "The 73rd Amendment added Part IX and the Eleventh Schedule; the 74th added Part IXA and the Twelfth Schedule.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which set of facts best explains why panchayats were weak in many states before 1992?", options: ["The Constitution forbade states from legislating on them", "They had no constitutional status, lacked funds, and their elections were often postponed for years", "They were placed on the Union List and ignored by Parliament", "The Gram Sabha had been abolished in 1957"], answer: 1, explain: "Local government was a State List subject, Article 40 was only a Directive Principle, and panchayats lacked funds and regular elections until they got constitutional status.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What did the PESA Act, 1996 do?", a: "It extended Panchayati Raj, with special safeguards, to the Scheduled (tribal) Areas.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the three types of urban local bodies created under the 74th Amendment.", a: "Nagar Panchayat, Municipal Council and Municipal Corporation.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give two reasons why local government is important for democracy.", a: "1. It is closest to the people, so local problems can be solved locally by people who know them best.\n2. It lets citizens participate directly in decisions and makes government more accountable (decentralisation strengthens democracy).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State the roles of the State Election Commission and the State Finance Commission in local government.", a: "1. The State Election Commission is an independent body that conducts elections to panchayats and municipalities.\n2. The State Finance Commission reviews the financial position of the local bodies every five years and recommends how funds should be shared with them.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why were panchayats weak in many states before the 73rd Amendment? Mention three points.", a: "1. Local government was a State List subject and Article 40 was only a Directive Principle, so states were not bound to strengthen panchayats.\n2. Panchayats lacked funds, and their elections were often postponed for years.\n3. Efforts such as the Balwant Rai Mehta Committee (1957) and the Community Development Programme did not give them lasting powers, so the L.M. Singhvi Committee (1986) recommended constitutional status.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the main provisions of the 74th Amendment.", a: "1. It added Part IXA and the Twelfth Schedule (18 subjects); it came into force on 1 June 1993.\n2. It created Nagar Panchayats, Municipal Councils and Municipal Corporations according to the size and nature of the urban area.\n3. It provides for direct elections, reservation for women, SCs and STs, and the State Election Commission and State Finance Commission, as in the 73rd Amendment.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How does decentralisation make democracy stronger? Refer to Gandhi's idea of gram swaraj.", a: "1. Decentralisation moves decisions to the level closest to the people, who can supervise elected representatives easily.\n2. It increases participation, e.g. through the Gram Sabha, and makes governments more accountable.\n3. Gandhi saw self-governing villages (gram swaraj) as the foundation of a decentralised democracy.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Compare the 73rd and 74th Amendments in terms of their coverage, structure and common features.", a: "1. Coverage: the 73rd Amendment deals with rural local government (panchayats) and the 74th with urban local government (municipalities).\n2. Constitutional additions: Part IX and the Eleventh Schedule (29 subjects) by the 73rd; Part IXA and the Twelfth Schedule (18 subjects) by the 74th.\n3. Structure: Gram Panchayat, Panchayat Samiti and Zila Panchayat by the 73rd; Nagar Panchayat, Municipal Council and Municipal Corporation by the 74th.\n4. Common features: direct elections every five years, State Election Commission, State Finance Commission, and reservation for women (at least one-third), SCs and STs.\n5. Both came into force in 1993 (24 April and 1 June respectively) and gave local bodies constitutional status.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Evaluate the working of local government in India since the 73rd and 74th Amendments.", a: "1. Regular elections have brought lakhs of elected representatives into local government.\n2. Large numbers of women, SCs and STs have entered local bodies because of reservations; many states reserve 50% of seats for women.\n3. The PESA Act, 1996 extended Panchayati Raj with safeguards to tribal areas.\n4. Challenges: local bodies raise little revenue of their own and depend on grants from state and Union governments.\n5. Many states have not transferred subjects, funds and staff, so bodies mostly implement schemes designed elsewhere; real decentralisation needs more financial and administrative autonomy.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: In a village, the panchayat wants to build a new drinking-water tank. Only a handful of villagers attended the meeting where the plan was discussed. The panchayat has almost no income of its own and is waiting for a grant from the state government. The post of sarpanch in this village is reserved for a woman.\n(i) Who are the members of the Gram Sabha, and why is it important?\n(ii) Under which Amendment is the reservation of the sarpanch's post for women provided?\n(iii) What does the dependence on a state grant show about local government?", a: "(i) All adult voters of the village panchayat area. It lets people take part directly, reviews the panchayat's work and makes it accountable; poor attendance weakens this.\n(ii) The 73rd Amendment, which reserves at least one-third of seats and chairperson posts for women.\n(iii) It shows that local bodies lack their own financial resources and depend on higher governments, so they have limited autonomy despite constitutional status.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "constitution-as-a-living-document": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The 61st Amendment (1988) lowered the voting age from 21 to:", options: ["16", "18", "19", "20"], answer: 1, explain: "The 61st Amendment reduced the voting age to 18, an amendment passed with wide political consensus.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the Golaknath case (1967), the Supreme Court held that Parliament:", options: ["can amend any part of the Constitution", "needs a referendum to amend it", "can amend only the Preamble", "cannot amend Fundamental Rights"], answer: 3, explain: "Golaknath held that Fundamental Rights cannot be amended; Parliament responded with the 24th Amendment (1971).", level: 1 },
      { q: "The National Emergency during which the 38th, 39th and 42nd Amendments were passed lasted from:", options: ["1962 to 1964", "1971 to 1973", "1975 to 1977", "1980 to 1982"], answer: 2, explain: "The Emergency of 1975–77 saw amendments that curbed the judiciary and strengthened the executive.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In the Indra Sawhney case (1992), the Supreme Court held that reservations should generally not exceed:", options: ["60%", "50%", "40%", "33%"], answer: 1, explain: "The Court set a general ceiling of 50% on reservations.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The 44th Amendment (1978) removed which right from the list of Fundamental Rights?", options: ["Right to property", "Right to equality", "Right to freedom of religion", "Right to education"], answer: 0, explain: "The 44th Amendment reversed many 42nd Amendment changes, restored the five-year term and removed the right to property from Fundamental Rights.", level: 1 },

      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Most amendments to the Indian Constitution have been about controversial political issues.\nReason (R): Many amendments were technical or administrative, such as the one that raised the retirement age of High Court judges.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A is false because most amendments are technical or not controversial; R is true (15th Amendment, 1963).", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "If the two Houses disagree on a Constitution amendment bill:", options: ["a joint sitting is called", "the Lok Sabha view prevails", "there is no joint sitting; the bill must pass each House separately by the required majority", "the bill goes to a referendum"], answer: 2, explain: "Amendment bills can be introduced only in Parliament and must be passed by each House separately; there is no joint sitting and no referendum.", level: 2 },
      { q: "After the 24th Amendment (1971), the President:", options: ["may withhold assent to a Constitution amendment bill", "must give assent to a Constitution amendment bill passed by Parliament", "can send it for a referendum", "needs the Supreme Court's approval before assenting"], answer: 1, explain: "The 24th Amendment made it obligatory for the President to give assent to an amendment bill.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Parliament wants to change the name of a state. Which route is correct?", options: ["An ordinary law under Article 3; it is not treated as an amendment under Article 368", "A special majority plus ratification by half the state legislatures", "A referendum in that state", "The consent of all states"], answer: 0, explain: "Changes of names and boundaries under Article 3 are made by ordinary law with a simple majority.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: Parliament proposes an amendment to the provisions on the distribution of legislative powers between the Union and the states. What is required?", options: ["A simple majority in the Lok Sabha", "A special majority in each House plus ratification by at least half of the state legislatures", "A special majority in each House only", "A special majority plus ratification by all state legislatures"], answer: 1, explain: "Federal provisions need the special majority and ratification by at least half of the state legislatures.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Which is the correct chronological order?", options: ["Kesavananda Bharati; Golaknath; 24th Amendment; 42nd Amendment; 44th Amendment", "Golaknath; Kesavananda Bharati; 24th Amendment; 44th Amendment; 42nd Amendment", "24th Amendment; Golaknath; Kesavananda Bharati; 44th Amendment; 42nd Amendment", "Golaknath (1967); 24th Amendment (1971); Kesavananda Bharati (1973); 42nd Amendment (1976); 44th Amendment (1978)"], answer: 3, explain: "1967 Golaknath, 1971 24th Amendment, 1973 Kesavananda Bharati, 1976 42nd Amendment, 1978 44th Amendment.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Parliament cannot, by an amendment, destroy the basic structure of the Constitution.\nReason (R): In Kesavananda Bharati (1973) the Supreme Court held that Parliament can amend any part of the Constitution but cannot destroy its basic structure.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The limit on Parliament's amending power comes directly from the holding in Kesavananda Bharati.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Reading the rights to privacy, shelter and a clean environment into Article 21 shows that the Constitution changes through:", options: ["formal amendment under Article 368", "an ordinance of the President", "judicial interpretation of the existing text", "a referendum"], answer: 2, explain: "Courts widened the meaning of Article 21 without changing its words, an example of change through interpretation.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Suppose an amendment, passed with the required majorities and state ratification, abolished judicial review altogether. According to the basic structure doctrine, it:", options: ["would be valid because it followed the Article 368 procedure", "can be struck down because judicial review is part of the basic structure", "would be valid because Parliament may amend any part without any limit", "would be invalid only if the President refused assent"], answer: 1, explain: "Judicial review is among the features treated as part of the basic structure, which even a properly passed amendment cannot destroy.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is a 'special majority' for amending the Constitution?", a: "A majority of the total membership of each House together with two-thirds of the members present and voting in that House.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the case in which the basic structure doctrine was laid down. How many judges decided it and by what majority?", a: "Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973), decided by a 13-judge bench by a 7–6 majority.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Why is the 42nd Amendment (1976) called a 'mini-constitution'? Give two of its changes.", a: "1. It made sweeping changes in many parts of the Constitution, hence the nickname.\n2. Examples: it added 'socialist', 'secular' and 'integrity' to the Preamble, added Fundamental Duties, extended the Lok Sabha term to 6 years and restricted judicial review (any two).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Give two examples of constitutional amendments that were passed with wide political consensus.", a: "1. The 61st Amendment (1988), which lowered the voting age to 18.\n2. The 73rd and 74th Amendments (1992) on local government (also the 52nd Amendment on anti-defection, 1985).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why has the Indian Constitution been amended so many times? Give three reasons.", a: "1. Many amendments are technical or administrative, e.g. the 15th Amendment (1963) raising the retirement age of High Court judges from 60 to 62.\n2. Some came from differing interpretations between Parliament and the judiciary, especially over property rights.\n3. Many reflected political consensus across parties, e.g. the 61st, 73rd and 74th Amendments; the most controversial ones came mainly between 1970 and 1990.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What changes did the 44th Amendment (1978) make after the Emergency?", a: "1. It reversed many of the changes made by the 42nd Amendment.\n2. It restored the five-year term of the Lok Sabha.\n3. It removed the right to property from the list of Fundamental Rights.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Trace the sequence from the Golaknath case to the Kesavananda Bharati case.", a: "1. In Golaknath (1967) the Supreme Court held that Parliament cannot amend Fundamental Rights.\n2. Parliament responded with the 24th Amendment (1971), asserting its power to amend any part and making the President's assent obligatory.\n3. In Kesavananda Bharati (1973) the Court held that Parliament can amend any part, but not destroy the basic structure.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how the basic structure doctrine evolved and why it is important.", a: "1. In Golaknath (1967), the Court said Fundamental Rights cannot be amended; the 24th Amendment (1971) followed.\n2. In Kesavananda Bharati (1973), a 13-judge bench, by 7–6, laid down that Parliament can amend any part but cannot destroy the basic structure.\n3. Features treated as basic include supremacy of the Constitution, democratic and republican government, secularism, federalism, separation of powers and judicial review.\n4. The doctrine was reaffirmed in the Minerva Mills case (1980) and is now accepted.\n5. It limits the amending power, so a temporary majority cannot alter the Constitution's core, and gives the judiciary a key role in protecting it.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Besides formal amendments, in what other ways does the Constitution change? Explain with examples.", a: "1. Judicial interpretation: courts widened Article 21 to include livelihood, shelter, a clean environment and privacy.\n2. Judicial rulings also set limits, e.g. Indra Sawhney (1992) held that reservations should generally not exceed 50%.\n3. Political practice and conventions shape how institutions work in practice.\n4. Formal amendments under Article 368 (more than 100) also adapt the text, with special majorities and state ratification for federal provisions.\n5. Together these let the Constitution respond to new situations while keeping its core intact, which is why it is called a living document.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Parliament passes an amendment bill on the election of the President with a special majority in both Houses. Later, another bill is passed that, according to critics, would abolish the power of courts to review laws. The matter is taken to the Supreme Court.\n(i) What additional step is needed for the first amendment?\n(ii) Why is there no joint sitting for amendment bills?\n(iii) On what basis can the Supreme Court examine the second amendment?", a: "(i) Ratification by at least half of the state legislatures, since the election of the President is a federal provision.\n(ii) Amendment bills must be passed by each House separately by the required majority; the Constitution gives no joint-sitting procedure to resolve disagreement on them.\n(iii) The basic structure doctrine from Kesavananda Bharati (1973): Parliament cannot destroy the basic structure, and judicial review is part of it.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "philosophy-of-the-constitution": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Untouchability is abolished by:", options: ["Article 14", "Article 15", "Article 17", "Article 21"], answer: 2, explain: "Article 17 abolishes untouchability, an example of the Constitution's commitment to social justice.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The right of minorities to establish and run educational institutions is given by:", options: ["Article 19", "Article 30", "Article 32", "Article 44"], answer: 1, explain: "Article 30 gives religious and linguistic minorities this community-based right.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Nehru Report (1928) had already demanded:", options: ["universal adult franchise and fundamental rights", "a separate electorate for every community", "an official state religion", "abolition of the princely states"], answer: 0, explain: "The idea of universal adult franchise came from the national movement; the Nehru Report demanded it in 1928.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Safeguards against arbitrary arrest are provided by:", options: ["Article 18", "Article 24", "Article 29", "Article 22"], answer: 3, explain: "Article 22 protects individual freedom against arbitrary arrest.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The Constituent Assembly Debates are useful for understanding the Constitution because they:", options: ["decide present-day elections", "list the members of the Lok Sabha", "explain the reasons behind the provisions of the Constitution", "give judges the power to amend it"], answer: 2, explain: "The Debates record the reasons behind each provision, helping us grasp the philosophy of the Constitution.", level: 1 },

      { q: "'Principled distance' in Indian secularism means that the state:", options: ["keeps a strict wall away from religion in all matters", "engages with or stays away from religion as needed to promote freedom and equality", "supports only the majority religion", "adopts one religion as official"], answer: 1, explain: "Indian secularism is not a strict wall of separation; the state intervenes when needed to promote equality.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Under Indian secularism the state can intervene in religious practices.\nReason (R): Indian secularism is based on a strict wall of separation between state and religion.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true (e.g. ending untouchability), but R is false: Indian secularism allows principled distance, not a strict wall.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A linguistic minority community runs a college and asks for state aid on the same terms as other colleges. Which statement fits the Constitution's approach?", options: ["Minorities may not run educational institutions", "Aid can go only to institutions of the majority community", "Aid must be refused to institutions run by communities", "Minorities may run such institutions under Article 30, and the state may aid them, treating all communities alike"], answer: 3, explain: "Article 30 recognises this community-based right, and the state can give aid to institutions run by different communities on an equal basis.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A temple refuses entry to people of some castes. The state passes a law opening it to all castes. This shows that:", options: ["the Constitution allows the state to reform religious practices to promote equality", "the state has stopped being secular", "freedom of religion has been abolished", "the state has adopted a religion"], answer: 0, explain: "Freedom of religion does not stop the state from reforming religion, e.g. opening temples to all castes and ending untouchability.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Dr Ambedkar warned that political equality without social and economic equality would leave India in:", options: ["a state of perpetual elections", "a state without a Supreme Court", "a life of contradictions", "an unfederal arrangement"], answer: 2, explain: "He feared a life of contradictions: equality in politics but inequality in social and economic life.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Which response best answers the criticism that the Constitution is an 'alien document'?", options: ["It copied foreign constitutions word for word, which was a strength", "The makers adapted ideas to Indian conditions after long debate, and many ideas came from the national movement", "It was approved by a national referendum", "Foreign constitutions were ignored entirely"], answer: 1, explain: "Borrowed ideas were adapted after long debate, and many ideas came from India's own national movement.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The Constitution has been criticised for a centralised idea of national unity.\nReason (R): It gives the Union strong powers over the states, for example through emergency provisions and the power under Article 3.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The strong powers of the Union are the basis for the criticism of centralisation.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Why did the makers combine individual freedoms with reservations for SCs and STs?", options: ["Because freedoms were not meant for all citizens", "Because liberalism requires identical treatment in all circumstances", "Because formal equality was not enough for historically disadvantaged groups, who needed special measures", "Because they wished to reduce the role of the legislature"], answer: 2, explain: "The makers linked liberalism with social justice; special measures were needed to achieve real equality.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a limitation of the Constitution mentioned in the chapter?", options: ["It denies minorities the right to run educational institutions", "It makes religion the basis of citizenship", "It allows only men to vote", "Some basic social and economic rights, such as the right to work, are only Directive Principles"], answer: 3, explain: "Besides a centralised idea of unity and neglect of some gender-justice issues, some social and economic rights are only Directive Principles.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is meant by 'principled distance' in Indian secularism?", a: "It is the approach in which the state engages with or stays away from religion, depending on what is needed to promote freedom and equality, instead of keeping a strict wall of separation.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What are community-based rights? Give one example from the Constitution.", a: "Community-based rights are rights given to groups, not just individuals. Example: Article 30 lets religious and linguistic minorities set up and run their own educational institutions.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "In what two ways is the Constitution a means of limiting power?", a: "1. It is a moral and legal framework that prevents governments from becoming tyrannical.\n2. It protects individuals through Fundamental Rights, such as safeguards against arbitrary arrest (Article 22), which courts can enforce.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What is asymmetric federalism? Give one example from the Constitution.", a: "1. It is a federal arrangement in which some states get special provisions because of their distinct histories and cultures.\n2. Example: special provisions under Article 371 for north-eastern states.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How does Indian secularism differ from a strict separation of state and religion?", a: "1. In strict separation, the state stays completely away from religion; Indian secularism keeps a principled distance.\n2. The state can intervene to promote equality, e.g. ending untouchability and opening temples to all castes, while Articles 25–28 protect freedom of religion.\n3. The state can aid educational institutions run by religious communities, treating all communities alike.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "What are the procedural achievements of the Constitution?", a: "1. It reflects faith in political deliberation: differences are settled by discussion and reasoned argument.\n2. The makers showed a spirit of accommodation and compromise and avoided deciding major issues by narrow majority votes.\n3. It is open to change through amendment, interpretation and practice, so each generation can adapt it.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why was the grant of universal adult franchise in 1950 significant?", a: "1. All adults got the vote from the very beginning, in 1950, while many older democracies extended voting rights only gradually.\n2. The idea came from the national movement; the Nehru Report (1928) had demanded it.\n3. It reflects the Constitution's faith in equal citizenship and democratic participation of all.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how the Constitution is both a means to limit power and an instrument of democratic transformation.", a: "1. The Constitution carries a moral vision (justice, liberty, equality, fraternity) and sets limits on the power of governments.\n2. Fundamental Rights, such as safeguards against arbitrary arrest, protect individuals from misuse of power.\n3. It also aims to change an unequal society: it abolishes untouchability (Article 17) and provides reservations for SCs and STs.\n4. Dr Ambedkar warned that political equality without social and economic equality would leave India in a life of contradictions.\n5. The transformation is to be achieved through peaceful, legal means and not by revolution.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "How does the Constitution accommodate India's diversity? Illustrate with community rights, secularism and federalism.", a: "1. Community-based rights: under Article 30 minorities can set up and run their own educational institutions.\n2. Secularism: Articles 25–28 protect freedom of religion, and the state keeps a principled distance, intervening to promote equality.\n3. Asymmetric federalism: special provisions such as Article 371 recognise distinct histories and cultures of some states.\n4. Common national identity: the Constitution builds one national identity while respecting regional, linguistic and religious identities, seeing no conflict between them.\n5. Universal adult franchise gives every citizen an equal political voice, whatever their community.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A minority community in a town runs a school and applies for state aid on the same terms as other schools. Meanwhile, the state passes a law opening a temple to all castes; some members of the temple trust object that the state should not interfere in religious matters.\n(i) Which Article gives the community its right to run the school?\n(ii) Can the state give it aid?\n(iii) How would the idea of principled distance respond to the objection of the trust?", a: "(i) Article 30.\n(ii) Yes, the state can give aid to educational institutions run by religious communities, treating all communities alike.\n(iii) Indian secularism does not keep a strict wall between state and religion; the state may intervene to promote equality, as when it opens temples to all castes and ends untouchability, while freedom of religion remains protected.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "political-theory-an-introduction": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Gandhi's 'Hind Swaraj', discussing the meaning of true freedom, dates from:", options: ["1905", "1909", "1919", "1930"], answer: 1, explain: "Hind Swaraj was written in 1909.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Karl Marx argued that:", options: ["monarchy is the best form of government", "freedom is more important than equality", "equality is as crucial as freedom", "tradition should never be questioned"], answer: 2, explain: "Marx held that equality is as important as freedom; Rousseau stressed freedom as a fundamental right.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of the following is a normative question?", options: ["How many seats did a party win?", "When was the Constitution adopted?", "What limits should be placed on the power of government?", "Which ministry looks after railways?"], answer: 2, explain: "A normative question asks what ought to be; the others ask for facts.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Socrates is remembered for:", options: ["writing the Leviathan", "founding a political party", "drafting a constitution", "questioning accepted beliefs and asking people to give reasons for them"], answer: 3, explain: "Like Socrates, political theorists question accepted beliefs and ask for reasons.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In 'The Republic' justice is discussed through the voice of Socrates by:", options: ["Plato", "Aristotle", "Locke", "Rousseau"], answer: 0, explain: "Plato wrote The Republic; Aristotle described humans as political animals.", level: 1 },

      { q: "People's acceptance that a government has the right to rule is called its:", options: ["sovereignty", "legitimacy", "jurisdiction", "tolerance"], answer: 1, explain: "Legitimacy is the acceptance of an authority's right to rule.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Studying political theory helps citizens make more informed choices.\nReason (R): Political theory clarifies concepts such as freedom and equality and tests the validity of arguments about them.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Clear concepts and tested arguments help citizens judge governments and policies.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: In a class, one student says equality means giving every child the same textbook; another says children from poor homes should get extra help. What does this show?", options: ["Political concepts have only one fixed meaning", "The dispute is settled by whoever argues loudest", "Only teachers can decide what equality means", "People understand a concept like equality differently, and reasoned argument is needed to settle it"], answer: 3, explain: "Political theory deals with such disagreements by using reasoned argument instead of prejudice or force.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A group debates whether the state should have the right to monitor private messages sent online. Why is this a matter for political theory?", options: ["Because only theorists may discuss it", "Because new situations raise new questions about freedom and privacy, which theory examines through reasoned argument", "Because technology has no political aspect", "Because the Constitution forbids such a debate"], answer: 1, explain: "Freedom on the internet and privacy are examples of new questions that keep political theory developing.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Aristotle called human beings 'political animals' because:", options: ["they prefer conflict to cooperation", "they are born to rule others", "they refuse to obey any authority", "they live in communities and must decide together how to live"], answer: 3, explain: "People live in communities and must make collective decisions about how to live together.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Which pairing of thinker and idea is correct?", options: ["Rousseau – untouchables as a minority", "Aristotle – Leviathan", "Ambedkar – special protection for untouchables as a minority", "Marx – Hind Swaraj"], answer: 2, explain: "Ambedkar argued that untouchables should be treated as a minority; Rousseau stressed freedom, Marx equality, Gandhi wrote Hind Swaraj.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Political theorists question accepted beliefs.\nReason (R): Political theory seeks to impose one fixed answer on all citizens.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "A is true, but R is false: political theory aims at reasoned argument, not fixed answers imposed on all.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Which example best shows how political ideas shaped the Indian Constitution?", options: ["The Constitution has no ideals, only technical rules", "It copied ideas without discussion", "Only lawyers influenced its text", "The Preamble promises liberty and equality, Fundamental Rights protect freedom, and Article 17 abolishes untouchability"], answer: 3, explain: "Ideas of freedom and equality, and Ambedkar's concern for the untouchables, are reflected in these provisions.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A student says: 'Politics is only dirty power games, so I need not study political theory.' Which reply uses the chapter's reasoning best?", options: ["You are right; politics concerns only politicians", "Politics is also the process of making collective decisions that affect everyone, and as a citizen you need political ideas to judge governments", "Political theory teaches how to win elections", "Politics can be avoided if one stays away from public life"], answer: 1, explain: "Politics is about collective decisions; Gandhi felt it surrounds us on all sides, and citizens must understand ideas to judge governments.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is political theory?", a: "Political theory is the systematic study of the ideas and principles, such as freedom, equality and justice, that shape constitutions, governments and social life.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Who wrote 'Hind Swaraj' and what does it discuss?", a: "Mahatma Gandhi wrote it in 1909; it discusses the meaning of true freedom or swaraj.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the two ways in which people look at politics.", a: "1. A negative view sees politics as manipulation, corruption and power games played by politicians.\n2. A broader view sees politics as the process of making collective decisions, in which people with different interests negotiate and arrive at choices for society.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What is a normative question? Give one example.", a: "1. A normative question asks what ought to be, i.e. about values and ideals, and not only about what is.\n2. Example: What makes a government legitimate? (or What does a just society look like?)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How did the ideas of Rousseau, Marx and Ambedkar contribute to political thinking about freedom and equality? Link one to the Indian Constitution.", a: "1. Rousseau argued that freedom is a fundamental right of humanity.\n2. Marx argued that equality is as crucial as freedom.\n3. Ambedkar argued that untouchables should be treated as a minority with special protection; this thinking is reflected in Article 17, which abolishes untouchability.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Why should ordinary citizens study political theory? Give three reasons.", a: "1. As citizens and voters, we need to understand political ideas to make informed choices and judge governments.\n2. It helps us examine our own ideas and prejudices and become more tolerant of other views.\n3. Ideas such as freedom, equality and justice affect our everyday lives, and political theory trains us to think about them clearly and logically.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "How do new situations raise new questions for political theory? Illustrate.", a: "1. Political concepts such as freedom and equality are understood differently by different people, so they need constant examination.\n2. New situations, such as freedom on the internet, privacy and the rights of future generations, raise questions that earlier thinkers did not face.\n3. Theory therefore keeps developing, using reasoned argument to address them.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain what is studied in political theory, with reference to concepts, thinkers and the testing of arguments.", a: "1. Political theory studies the ideas and principles that shape constitutions, governments and social life in a systematic manner.\n2. It clarifies the meaning of concepts such as freedom, equality, justice, democracy and secularism.\n3. It studies how thinkers from Plato and Aristotle to Rousseau, Marx, Gandhi and Ambedkar understood these concepts.\n4. It tests the validity of arguments through reasoning and asks normative questions, e.g. what makes a government legitimate.\n5. It applies these ideas to present-day issues and helps us decide on them by reasoned argument and not by prejudice or force.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "'Political theory is not mere opinion.' Discuss.", a: "1. Opinions may rest on prejudice; political theory asks for reasons and systematic arguments.\n2. Like Socrates, theorists question accepted beliefs and ask people to justify them.\n3. It clarifies the meaning of concepts so that we argue about the same thing.\n4. It examines the validity of arguments made for ideas such as freedom and equality, drawing on thinkers like Rousseau and Marx.\n5. It encourages tolerance of other views and settles disputes by reasoned argument, not force.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Students of a school debate whether all children should be given exactly the same facilities or whether children who are disadvantaged should receive extra help. One student says this is just a matter of opinion and cannot be decided by argument.\n(i) Which political concept is being debated?\n(ii) How would political theory approach the disagreement?\n(iii) Why is it useful for students to take part in such a debate?", a: "(i) Equality.\n(ii) It would clarify what equality means (the same treatment or extra help for the disadvantaged), examine the reasons offered for each view, and test the validity of the arguments.\n(iii) It helps them examine their own ideas and prejudices, practise reasoned argument, become more tolerant of other views and prepare for informed citizenship.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  freedom: {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Aung San Suu Kyi, in 'Freedom from Fear', argued that:", options: ["only nations, not individuals, can be free", "fear prevents people from living with dignity", "freedom has no value in a poor country", "elections are the only form of freedom"], answer: 1, explain: "She argued that fear prevents people from living with dignity.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The nationalist who declared that swaraj was his birthright was:", options: ["Gokhale", "Nehru", "Tilak", "Ambedkar"], answer: 2, explain: "Tilak declared swaraj, meaning self-rule, to be his birthright.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The distinction between negative and positive liberty was made famous by:", options: ["J.S. Mill", "Isaiah Berlin", "Rousseau", "Gandhi"], answer: 1, explain: "Isaiah Berlin made the distinction famous.", level: 1 },
      { q: "J.S. Mill's 'On Liberty' was published in:", options: ["1789", "1909", "1950", "1859"], answer: 3, explain: "On Liberty (1859) contains the harm principle.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In India, Article 19(2) allows reasonable restrictions on:", options: ["freedom of speech and expression", "the right to vote", "abolition of untouchability", "the right to life"], answer: 0, explain: "The Constitution permits reasonable restrictions on freedoms such as free speech.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Which of the following is most clearly a self-regarding action in Mill's sense?", options: ["Playing loud music at midnight in a crowded neighbourhood", "Choosing what hobby to take up in one's free time", "Driving dangerously in a busy street", "Spreading false rumours about a neighbour"], answer: 1, explain: "A self-regarding action affects only the person who does it, and society should not interfere with it.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Case: A state introduces free school meals and scholarships so that poor children can attend school. This is mainly an example of:", options: ["negative liberty alone", "the harm principle", "swaraj as freedom from foreign rule", "positive liberty, which expands the conditions for self-development"], answer: 3, explain: "Positive liberty is about the conditions, such as education and health care, that enable people to develop.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Negative liberty is concerned with an area of non-interference.\nReason (R): Positive liberty is concerned with expanding the conditions for self-development.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both statements are true, but R does not explain A: they describe two different kinds of liberty.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Case: A resident plays very loud music at 2 a.m. and the neighbours cannot sleep. Mill's harm principle would allow restricting this because it is:", options: ["a self-regarding action", "disliked by the majority", "an other-regarding action that harms others", "music, which is always harmful"], answer: 2, explain: "The harm principle allows restriction when an other-regarding action causes harm to others.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In Gandhi's 'Hind Swaraj', true swaraj involves:", options: ["self-mastery and freedom from domination, not only an end to British rule", "only a change of rulers", "only economic growth", "only military strength"], answer: 0, explain: "Swaraj meant rule over oneself as well as freedom from foreign rule.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Two habits cause harm to others: one a mild annoyance, the other serious injury. Which response is consistent with Mill?", options: ["Social disapproval for the mild harm, legal restraint for the serious harm", "Legal punishment for both", "No response to either, since all actions are free", "Legal punishment for the mild harm, social disapproval for the serious harm"], answer: 0, explain: "Mill held that minor harm should meet only social disapproval, while serious harm may be restrained by law.", level: 3 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Negative liberty regards the state as the only source of freedom.\nReason (R): Positive liberty sees the state as able to enable freedom through education and health care.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "A is false: negative liberty sees interference, including by the state, as a threat. R is true.", level: 3, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A film is banned because some groups are offended by it. Which argument from Mill best opposes the ban?", options: ["Banning protects truth because false ideas have no value", "Ideas that offend can never contain truth", "A ban removes the clash of opposing views through which truth emerges, and is only a short-term solution", "Only the views of the majority deserve protection"], answer: 2, explain: "Mill argued that truth emerges from conflict of opposing views and that what seems false today may be true tomorrow.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which statement is consistent with the chapter?", options: ["Freedom means the absence of all constraints, so every law reduces it", "Social and economic inequalities such as caste or extreme poverty can restrict people's choices as much as force", "Constraints come only from the state and never from society", "Reasonable restrictions under Article 19(2) can never be justified"], answer: 1, explain: "Constraints arise from domination and also from social and economic inequalities; some constraints are justified to prevent harm.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State J.S. Mill's harm principle.", a: "Society or the state can restrict a person's freedom against their will only to prevent harm to others.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is swaraj?", a: "Swaraj means self-rule: freedom from foreign rule and also rule over oneself, i.e. self-mastery and freedom from domination.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What are the two aspects of freedom discussed in the chapter?", a: "1. The absence of external constraints, i.e. not being unnecessarily controlled by others.\n2. The presence of conditions in which people can develop their talents and take decisions about their own lives.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Distinguish between self-regarding and other-regarding actions, with one example of each.", a: "1. A self-regarding action affects only the person who does it (e.g. choosing one's hobby); society should not interfere.\n2. An other-regarding action affects others (e.g. playing loud music at night); restrictions can be justified if it causes harm.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What are the sources of constraints on freedom?", a: "1. Domination and external control, such as force, unjust laws, dictatorship or colonial rule.\n2. Social and economic inequality, such as the caste system and extreme poverty, which limit people's choices.\n3. Some constraints are needed to maintain order and prevent harm to others, so the question is which constraints are justified.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Give Mill's reasons for protecting freedom of expression.", a: "1. No idea is completely false; even ideas we consider false usually contain some truth.\n2. Truth emerges only from the conflict of opposing views, and challenges keep true ideas alive.\n3. We cannot be sure that what is considered false today will not be seen as true tomorrow.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Are all constraints on freedom bad? Explain with reference to Article 19(2).", a: "1. No; some constraints are needed to maintain order and to prevent harm to others.\n2. The important question is which constraints are justified; in a democracy they should be decided through reasoned debate and laws that people accept.\n3. The Constitution permits reasonable restrictions on freedoms such as free speech under Article 19(2).", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain negative and positive liberty with examples and say why both are needed.", a: "1. Negative liberty ('freedom from') defines an area of non-interference in which no external authority, including the state, can interfere.\n2. Example: freedom of thought and expression; the larger this area, the greater the freedom.\n3. Positive liberty ('freedom to') is about expanding the conditions for self-development.\n4. Example: the state promoting freedom through education and health care. The distinction was made famous by Isaiah Berlin.\n5. Both are needed: protection from interference, and real opportunities to use one's freedom.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Discuss the individual and collective aspects of freedom with reference to swaraj.", a: "1. In India's freedom struggle, freedom was expressed as swaraj, meaning self-rule; Tilak declared it his birthright.\n2. Collective side: a nation free from British rule.\n3. Individual side: rule over oneself, self-control and freedom from domination, so that people can make their own choices.\n4. In Hind Swaraj (1909), Gandhi argued that true swaraj requires people to master themselves and not be dominated even by modern civilisation.\n5. Freedom therefore means both freedom for the nation and the conditions for individuals to develop and decide their own lives.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A film is banned in a state because some groups say it hurts their feelings. Supporters of the ban say it avoids trouble. Others say that a free society must tolerate views it dislikes within reasonable limits.\n(i) Which freedom is involved?\n(ii) Give one argument of Mill against such a ban.\n(iii) Under which provision can the state restrict this freedom?", a: "(i) Freedom of speech and expression, at the heart of the area of non-interference.\n(ii) Truth emerges from the conflict of opposing views, and what is considered false today may be seen as true tomorrow; banning to avoid offence is only a short-term solution.\n(iii) Article 19(2), which allows reasonable restrictions on grounds laid down in the Constitution.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
